\chapter{Daemons and services}
\label{ch:daemons}

A \emph{daemon} is a program that runs in the background and does a job for others: it waits for connections, writes a log, or
sends a heartbeat. Up to now Ulix has two kinds of such jobs, and both are part of the kernel: the network thread
(\texttt{netd}) and the servers that run in it (\texttt{telnetd}, \texttt{sshd}). That was the shortcut of the first steps. A
server in the kernel can crash the system, its mistakes cost kernel memory, and the SSH server's cryptography has to fit on the
16~KByte stack of a kernel thread. A server should be a \emph{program}: it can be written, tested, started, stopped and killed
like any other, and when it fails, only it fails.

What is missing is not the mechanism --- \texttt{fork}, \texttt{exec} and \texttt{kill} are enough --- but a \emph{convention}: how
are services described, who starts them when the machine boots, how does the administrator start and stop one, and how does one
find out whether it is running? The BSDs answer this with \texttt{/etc/rc}, the scripts in \texttt{/etc/rc.d}, the file
\texttt{/etc/rc.conf} and the command \texttt{service}. Ulix takes the same names and the same ideas, and leaves out what needs a
shell language: where a BSD service is a script, a Ulix service is a \emph{data file}.

\section{The convention}

\begin{description}
\item[\texttt{/etc/rc.conf}] says which services are switched on, one line each: \texttt{heartbeat\_enable="YES"}. This is the file the
  kernel services already use (\texttt{telnetd\_enable}, \texttt{sshd\_enable}, \texttt{tls\_enable}).
\item[\texttt{/etc/rc.d/NAME}] describes the service \texttt{NAME} in lines of the same form: \texttt{command} (the program;
  its arguments may follow), \texttt{rcvar} (the name of its switch in \texttt{rc.conf}), \texttt{default} (\texttt{YES} or
  \texttt{NO}: what to do if \texttt{rc.conf} does not mention the switch) and \texttt{pidfile} (the default is
  \texttt{/var/run/NAME.pid}). Lines that start with \texttt{\#} are comments.
\item[\texttt{daemon}] starts a program in the background: it detaches the program from the terminal it was started on and
  writes its process number into the pid file. (The name and the job are those of \texttt{daemon(8)} of FreeBSD.)
\item[\texttt{service}] is the administrator's tool: \texttt{service NAME start}, \texttt{stop}, \texttt{restart},
  \texttt{status}; \texttt{service -e} lists the services that are switched on, \texttt{service -l} all of them, and
  \texttt{service -a start} starts every service that is switched on.
\item[boot] The kernel runs \texttt{service -a start} once, after the file system is mounted and before the first \texttt{login}
  appears. This is the whole job of \texttt{/etc/rc} in this system.
\end{description}

\section{The pid file}

A service that runs in the background has no terminal that could tell the administrator what it is doing, and the shell does not
wait for it. The only handle on it is its process number, and it is kept in a file: \texttt{/var/run/NAME.pid} holds the number
as text. \texttt{service stop} reads it and sends the signal; \texttt{service status} reads it and asks the kernel whether that
process is alive (\texttt{kill} with signal 0 delivers nothing and only tells whether the process exists; a process of another user, for example a daemon
that the boot started for root, exists even when the answer is ``not permitted'': only its owner and root may stop it).

The method has a weakness every Unix has: if the machine ended without \texttt{stop}, the file is left behind, and a later
process may have the old number. \texttt{service} then thinks the service is running. The directories are on the disk, and the disk
is not emptied at boot; \texttt{service -a start} therefore does not trust a pid file blindly --- a service is only called running
when the number belongs to a living process --- but it cannot tell \emph{which} program that process is. For a teaching system that
is acceptable; the book of BSD has the same sentence.

\section{\texttt{daemon}: detach and note the number}

\texttt{daemon [-p pidfile] command [arguments]} does three things. It starts the command in a child process. It moves the child to
the last virtual terminal (number 9, which nobody logs in on), so the keys and the Ctrl-C of the terminal the administrator uses do
not reach it: on Ulix the processes of a terminal are its jobs (\texttt{console\_interrupt}), and a daemon is not a job. And it
writes the process number of the child into the pid file and ends; the child's parent is gone, so the system's first process adopts it,
and nobody has to wait for it.

What the daemon prints is shown on terminal 9 (Alt-F10 on the console). A daemon with a lot to say should write a log file.

<<userland/daemon.c>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"
#include "../arm64-extra.h"

#define LOG_TERMINAL 9                         // the terminal that no user logs in on: the daemon's output goes there

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  char *pidfile = 0;
  int first = 1;
  if (argc > 2 && strcmp (argv[1], "-p") == 0) { pidfile = argv[2];  first = 3; }
  if (first >= argc) { printf ("usage: daemon [-p pidfile] command [arguments]\n");  return 1; }

  mkdir ("/var", 0755);                        // (fails if they exist: fine)
  mkdir ("/var/run", 0755);

  int pid = fork ();
  if (pid < 0) { printf ("daemon: cannot fork\n");  return 1; }
  if (pid == 0) {
    setterm (LOG_TERMINAL);                    // leave the terminal of the administrator
    execv (argv[first], &argv[first]);
    printf ("daemon: cannot start %s\n", argv[first]);
    exit (1);
  }
  if (pidfile) {
    unlink (pidfile);                          // (a new file: there is no O_TRUNC yet)
    int fd = open (pidfile, O_WRONLY | O_CREAT, 0644);
    if (fd < 0) { printf ("daemon: cannot write %s\n", pidfile);  kill (pid, SIGKILL);  return 1; }
    char text[16];
    sprintf (text, "%d\n", pid);
    write (fd, text, strlen (text));
    close (fd);
  }
  return 0;
}
@

\section{\texttt{service}}

\texttt{service} has four small jobs, and each is a function of at most a screenful. The first is reading text lines of the form
\texttt{name="value"} from a file, which serves for \texttt{rc.conf} and for the files in \texttt{rc.d} alike:
\texttt{get\_setting (text, name, out)} looks for a line that begins with \texttt{name=}, removes the quotes and copies the rest. Both files
are small, so the whole file is read into a buffer first.

<<userland/service.c: settings>>=
#define MAXTEXT 2048

// reads a whole (small) file into text; 0 if it cannot be opened
static int read_file (const char *path, char *text) {
  int fd = open (path, O_RDONLY, 0), n = 0, k;
  if (fd < 0) return 0;
  while (n < MAXTEXT - 1 && (k = read (fd, text + n, MAXTEXT - 1 - n)) > 0) n += k;
  close (fd);
  text[n] = 0;
  return 1;
}

// finds a line  name="value"  (the quotes are optional) at the start of a line; 1 if found
static int get_setting (const char *text, const char *name, char *value) {
  int len = strlen (name);
  for (const char *p = text; *p; ) {
    if (strncmp (p, name, len) == 0 && p[len] == '=') {
      const char *v = p + len + 1;
      if (*v == '"') v++;
      int i = 0;
      while (v[i] && v[i] != '"' && v[i] != '\n' && i < 127) { value[i] = v[i];  i++; }
      value[i] = 0;
      return 1;
    }
    while (*p && *p != '\n') p++;              // next line
    if (*p) p++;
  }
  return 0;
}
@

The second job is to put together what is known about one service: the file in \texttt{rc.d}, the switch in \texttt{rc.conf} and the
state of the process. A service that has no file in \texttt{rc.d} does not exist.

<<userland/service.c: services>>=
struct service {
  char name[32], command[128], pidfile[128], rcvar[64];
  int  enabled;                                // switched on in rc.conf (or by its default)
};

static int load_service (const char *name, struct service *s) {
  char path[64], text[MAXTEXT], value[128];
  sprintf (path, "/etc/rc.d/%s", name);
  if (!read_file (path, text)) return 0;
  strncpy (s->name, name, 31);  s->name[31] = 0;
  if (!get_setting (text, "command", s->command)) return 0;
  if (!get_setting (text, "pidfile", s->pidfile)) sprintf (s->pidfile, "/var/run/%s.pid", name);
  if (!get_setting (text, "rcvar", s->rcvar)) sprintf (s->rcvar, "%s_enable", name);
  int enabled = 0;                             // the default of the service, unless rc.conf says something
  if (get_setting (text, "default", value)) enabled = strcmp (value, "YES") == 0;
  char conf[MAXTEXT];
  if (read_file ("/etc/rc.conf", conf) && get_setting (conf, s->rcvar, value)) enabled = strcmp (value, "YES") == 0;
  s->enabled = enabled;
  return 1;
}

// does the process exist? Signal 0 is not delivered, kill only tells whether it could be. A process of another user (a daemon that
// the boot started for root) exists, too, even though we may not signal it: that is the answer "not permitted", not "no such process".
static int alive (int pid) { return kill (pid, 0) == 0 || errno == EPERM; }

// the process number of the service, or 0 if it does not run (no pid file, or no such process)
static int running_pid (struct service *s) {
  char text[32];
  if (!read_file (s->pidfile, text)) return 0;
  int pid = atoi (text);
  return pid > 2 && alive (pid) ? pid : 0;
}
@

The third job is to start and to stop. Starting runs \texttt{daemon} and waits for it (it ends at once). Stopping sends \texttt{SIGTERM}, the
polite request to end, and waits up to three seconds for the process to disappear; a program that does not answer is then
killed with \texttt{SIGKILL}. A process only reacts to \texttt{SIGTERM} if it has a handler (otherwise the kernel kills it, which
works but is noisy), so a well-behaved daemon installs one --- see \texttt{heartbeat} below.

<<userland/service.c: start and stop>>=
static void wait_ms (int ms) {                 // the clock is read in microseconds; other processes run meanwhile
  unsigned start = clock_us ();
  while ((clock_us () - start) / 1000 < (unsigned)ms) resign ();
}

static int service_start (struct service *s) {
  int pid = running_pid (s);
  if (pid) { printf ("%s is already running (pid %d).\n", s->name, pid);  return 0; }
  char *argv[8];
  int n = 0;
  argv[n++] = "daemon";  argv[n++] = "-p";  argv[n++] = s->pidfile;
  char *c = s->command;                        // the command and its arguments, separated by blanks
  while (*c && n < 7) {
    argv[n++] = c;
    while (*c && *c != ' ') c++;
    if (*c) *c++ = 0;
  }
  argv[n] = 0;
  int child = fork (), status = 0;
  if (child == 0) { execv ("/bin/daemon", argv);  exit (1); }
  waitpid (child, &status, 0);
  if (status != 0 || !(pid = running_pid (s))) { printf ("Starting %s failed.\n", s->name);  return 1; }
  printf ("Starting %s (pid %d).\n", s->name, pid);
  return 0;
}

static int service_stop (struct service *s) {
  int pid = running_pid (s);
  if (!pid) { printf ("%s is not running.\n", s->name);  return 0; }
  if (kill (pid, SIGTERM) != 0) { printf ("service: cannot stop %s (pid %d): permission denied\n", s->name, pid);  return 1; }
  for (int i = 0; i < 30 && alive (pid); i++) wait_ms (100);
  if (alive (pid)) { kill (pid, SIGKILL);  wait_ms (300);  printf ("%s did not end and was killed.\n", s->name); }
  else printf ("Stopped %s.\n", s->name);
  unlink (s->pidfile);
  return 0;
}

static int service_status (struct service *s) {
  int pid = running_pid (s);
  if (pid) printf ("%s is running as pid %d.\n", s->name, pid);
  else     printf ("%s is not running.\n", s->name);
  return pid ? 0 : 1;                          // like the BSDs: the exit status says it
}
@

The fourth job is the loop over all services (\texttt{-a}, \texttt{-e}, \texttt{-l}): the names are the entries of the directory
\texttt{/etc/rc.d}.

<<userland/service.c: main>>=
static int for_all (int only_enabled, int (*action) (struct service *), int show_enabled) {
  struct dir_entry e;
  int result = 0;
  for (int index = 0; getdent ("/etc/rc.d", index, &e) != -1; index++) {
    if (e.filename[0] == '.' || e.inode == 0) continue;
    struct service s;
    if (!load_service ((char *)e.filename, &s)) continue;
    if (only_enabled && !s.enabled) continue;
    if (action) result |= action (&s);
    if (show_enabled) printf ("%s%s\n", s.name, s.enabled ? "" : "   (off)");
  }
  return result;
}

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  if (argc == 2 && strcmp (argv[1], "-e") == 0) return for_all (1, 0, 1);
  if (argc == 2 && strcmp (argv[1], "-l") == 0) return for_all (0, 0, 1);
  if (argc == 3 && strcmp (argv[1], "-a") == 0) {
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "start") == 0)  return for_all (1, service_start, 0);
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "stop") == 0)   return for_all (1, service_stop, 0);
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "status") == 0) return for_all (1, service_status, 0);
  }
  if (argc == 3) {
    struct service s;
    if (!load_service (argv[1], &s)) { printf ("service: %s: no such service (see /etc/rc.d)\n", argv[1]);  return 1; }
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "start") == 0)   return service_start (&s);
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "stop") == 0)    return service_stop (&s);
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "status") == 0)  return service_status (&s);
    if (strcmp (argv[2], "restart") == 0) { service_stop (&s);  return service_start (&s); }
  }
  printf ("usage: service name start|stop|restart|status\n"
          "       service -e | -l\n"
          "       service -a start|stop|status\n");
  return 2;
}
@

<<userland/service.c>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"
#include "../arm64-extra.h"

<<userland/service.c: settings>>
<<userland/service.c: services>>
<<userland/service.c: start and stop>>
<<userland/service.c: main>>
@

\section{A first service: \texttt{heartbeat}}

A convention needs at least one citizen that follows it, and the tests need one that does something visible. \texttt{heartbeat}
appends a line to \texttt{/var/log/heartbeat.log} every second. That is all, and it shows what every daemon has to get right:
it runs forever, it opens its log for each line and closes it again (so the file is complete if the machine stops), and it
reacts to \texttt{SIGTERM} by ending in an orderly way.

The waiting uses the system call that the program \texttt{top} uses, a wait for a key press with a time limit; on terminal 9, where a daemon
lives, nobody types, so it is a plain sleep.

<<userland/heartbeat.c>>=
#include "../ulixlib.h"
#include "../arm64-extra.h"

static volatile int stop = 0;
static void on_term (int signo) { stop = 1; }          // SIGTERM: end after the current round

int main (int argc, char **argv) {
  signal (SIGTERM, on_term);
  mkdir ("/var", 0755);
  mkdir ("/var/log", 0755);
  for (int n = 1; !stop; n++) {
    int fd = open ("/var/log/heartbeat.log", O_WRONLY | O_CREAT | O_APPEND, 0644);
    if (fd >= 0) {
      char line[32];
      sprintf (line, "heartbeat %d\n", n);
      write (fd, line, strlen (line));
      close (fd);
    }
    waitkey (1000);                                    // one second
  }
  return 0;
}
@

Its description is the file \texttt{/etc/rc.d/heartbeat}. It is switched off unless \texttt{rc.conf} says
\texttt{heartbeat\_enable="YES"}.

<<etc/rc.d/heartbeat>>=
# heartbeat: writes a line to /var/log/heartbeat.log every second (a test and an example for services)
command="/bin/heartbeat"
rcvar="heartbeat_enable"
default="NO"
pidfile="/var/run/heartbeat.pid"
@

\section{Starting the services at boot}

In \texttt{console\_main} (chapter on the boot) the kernel starts \texttt{/bin/service} with the arguments \texttt{-a start} before it starts
\texttt{/init}, and waits until that process has ended; the daemons it started stay (their parent, \texttt{service}, is gone, and the first process
adopts them). The kernel only does this in the build with login (the interactive build for the board and the one for the shell test), not
in the build for the kernel tests, which count processes.

\section{What is not done}

\begin{itemize}
\item No \texttt{rc.shutdown}: \texttt{shutdown} and \texttt{reboot} do not stop the services first. They end the machine; a daemon that writes to
  a file only has the disk buffers of the book to lose, and \texttt{sync} runs.
\item No dependencies between services (BSD's \texttt{REQUIRE} and \texttt{rcorder}): \texttt{-a start} goes through the directory in the order
  of the entries.
\item The pid file cannot tell \emph{which} program has the number (see above).
\item No restart of a daemon that has crashed (that would be a job for a supervisor, as in \texttt{daemontools} or \texttt{runit}).
\end{itemize}
